e-528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the differential equation $f'(x) - 3\\sin(2x) = 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/example-528.json" "file_sha256" "3a32590f00e2c420f013da8f3a4737a8b530181d2ef9d897f50ac11c68eb5370" "basis" 1154 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 3\\sin{2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/example-528.json" "file_sha256" "3a32590f00e2c420f013da8f3a4737a8b530181d2ef9d897f50ac11c68eb5370" "basis" 1154 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} = x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/example-529.json" "file_sha256" "e0189347ff677cac5ac0cfa1cd65796536d6ac8e035e18481ce2442d9eb3e72f" "basis" 1154 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-529" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+Cx+D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$ We cannot combine them, because $C$ is being multiplied by $x$ while $D$ is not." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/example-529.json" "file_sha256" "e0189347ff677cac5ac0cfa1cd65796536d6ac8e035e18481ce2442d9eb3e72f" "basis" 1154 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the differential equation $y'  = 2x,$ and sketch the solution curves of this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/example-530.json" "file_sha256" "d05449a953918ff7698cecd441b8eb5220ee5867a5257d998d8cdfb7054d114a" "basis" 1154 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\ny = x^2+C.\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/assets/e37771799ef5fadc259e6ea0073042a83e0974b5a2619d1af3e87a7467e6d780.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/example-530.json" "file_sha256" "d05449a953918ff7698cecd441b8eb5220ee5867a5257d998d8cdfb7054d114a" "basis" 1154 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the particular solution to the differential equation below that satisfies the condition $f(0)=2.$\n\n$$\nf'(x) - \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2} = 0\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/example-531.json" "file_sha256" "5a875f1ec40961e0f37948d29a9fef85d57f5517e1aa46a89e350d8254139301" "basis" 1154 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-531" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{3}{1+x^2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =3\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $f(x) = 3\\arctan{x} + C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $f(0) = 2$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C \\\\ 2 & =3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)+C \\\\ 2 & =0+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is \n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\arctan x + 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/example-531.json" "file_sha256" "5a875f1ec40961e0f37948d29a9fef85d57f5517e1aa46a89e350d8254139301" "basis" 1154 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029950/state.json" "file_sha256" "33d31df5c002d4c09c8da6a49f2643388148a2099bf31895c57ea2ce62f4fc26" "basis" 1203 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =3x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029950/assets/64b0962fbb38b2c64da68de01d1c6407174aa66f810b820ece7185866b7f8ff3.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029950/state.json" "file_sha256" "33d31df5c002d4c09c8da6a49f2643388148a2099bf31895c57ea2ce62f4fc26" "basis" 1203 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029950/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5577438eadf86b79813dc03e2fd7e3c8cd6ef835ab14f35430977dbeeac7846d" "basis" 1203 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029950/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-79563" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type"